### 5\. The actual workload behind 70 emails

I want to be realistic about this. Seventy personalised emails are a substantial amount of work.

If you spend approximately 45–90 minutes researching each professor, that is around 52–105 hours of research alone. Add drafting, proofreading, document preparation and programme searches, and the workload becomes significant.

I suggest using a tiered process:

| Professor category               | Research effort      | Email approach                                          |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Direct supervoid/void researcher | 60–90 min            | Detailed research connection                            |
| Closely related cosmologist      | 45–60 min            | Connect through a specific paper or method              |
| Broader astrophysics researcher  | 30–45 min            | Focus on a specific research overlap                    |
| Programme contact/coordinator    | Depends on programme | Ask about admission procedure, not research supervision |

These are planning estimates, not a substitute for understanding the work.

One adjustment I strongly recommend: keep 70 as your target, but maintain a separate count of qualified emails. An email counts as qualified only if you have verified the recipient, read relevant research, established a genuine connection and checked the application route.

It is better to have 45 strong emails than 70 messages that do not demonstrate research interest.

